    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Tunnel</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>NetName</string>
          <string>CAS_CC</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-1300" y="-120"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>textFontSize</string>
          <int>17</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>Two flip-flops derive most of the CGA&apos;s clocks by division, feeding outputs back into inputs. 
Each loop divides the source clock by 2.</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-1160" y="-560"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>textFontSize</string>
          <int>17</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>This XOR gate combines several of the CGA&apos;s clocks in 
order to synthesize the RAS and CAS signals for RAM.</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-1760" y="-560"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>textFontSize</string>
          <int>17</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>U46 is responsible from taking inputs from U47 and U1 and synthesizing
the ~ENRAS/~ENCAS signals, which are used to enable the relevant address
bus drivers.

It is important that only one bus driver drive the data bus at any given time.</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-3060" y="1620"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Rectangle</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>RectHeight</string>
          <int>36</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>RectInside</string>
          <boolean>true</boolean>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Label</string>
          <string>U48 &amp; U49: MA12 control, Shift-Load Generation</string>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>RectWidth</string>
          <int>39</int>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-820" y="1580"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>U30C</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-240" y="1980"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>textFontSize</string>
          <int>17</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>These two gates handle a variety of functions. 
One output is MA12, which utlizes the GRPH, ~GRPH, and RA0 inputs to determine
whether MA12 should be set - this creates the famous 8k offset for odd scanlines.

U49 also generates the pre-Shift Load signal, SL&apos; used for rasterization timing.</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-800" y="1620"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Rectangle</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>RectHeight</string>
          <int>36</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>RectInside</string>
          <boolean>true</boolean>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Label</string>
          <string>U50-U57: Video Memory</string>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>RectWidth</string>
          <int>39</int>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-20" y="1580"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>textFontSize</string>
          <int>17</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>Normally comprised of 8 bit addressable DRAM modules, I&apos;ve implemented VRAM
as a single 8 bit module for simplicity (and speed). It otherwise works exactly the same.

RAM is addressed by setting a 7 bit row address via the Row Address Strobe (RAS)
followed by a 7 bit column address set via Column Address Strobe (CAS). 

The ~WE pin controls whether the data presented on D is written, or if the stored data
is emitted on Q.  Since we control ~RAS and ~CAS with timed enable signals, we can 
tie these to the same bus.</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="0" y="1620"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Rectangle</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>RectHeight</string>
          <int>39</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>RectInside</string>
          <boolean>true</boolean>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Label</string>
          <string>U33: Character ROM</string>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>RectWidth</string>
          <int>26</int>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-540" y="320"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>textFontSize</string>
          <int>17</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>U27 has the critical role of generating the Dot and
Character Clocks, switching between the pairs of
14Mhz / HCLK and 7Mhz / LCLK depending on the
 status of the HRES signal.</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
